---
// Spoke (Phase 4): CMU block wall vs wood fence -- comparison/decision
// intent. $ figures from src/lib/costs.ts only (the SAME wood/vinyl/CMU
// ranges the cost guide uses -- deliberately not a second source's
// different ranges, lessons-learned #5). Lifespans from costs.ts `lifespan`
// (Angi Jul 2026; HomeAdvisor Jun 2026). Upkeep: HomeGuide fence cost
// (sealing every 1-2 years, Feb 3 2026) + HomeGuide stain cost (Feb 4 2026).
// Noise: FHWA (updated Jun 9 2017), highway-barrier context stated.
// Local rules: St. George City Code 10-18-1 (open-fence rule near sidewalk)
// and Ivins City Code 16.11.135 (CMU color on arterials), read 2026-09-26.
// Umbrella Service. Article + FAQ.
import GuideLayout from '../components/layout/GuideLayout.astro';
import IconList from '../components/sections/IconList.astro';
import { COST_AS_OF, perFoot, blockWall, fenceStain, lifespan } from '../lib/costs';

const path = '/cmu-block-wall-vs-wood-fence-st-george-ut/';
const wood = perFoot.find((r) => r.key === 'wood')!;
const vinyl = perFoot.find((r) => r.key === 'vinyl')!;

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Is a block wall more expensive than a wood fence?',
    answer: `Usually, up front. As of ${COST_AS_OF}, a wood privacy fence runs about ${wood.range} per linear foot installed, while a 6-foot CMU block wall works out to roughly ${blockWall.perLinear6ft} per linear foot (${blockWall.perSqFt} per square foot of wall). The gap narrows over time because block needs far less upkeep and lasts much longer.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a block wall last compared with a wood fence?',
    answer: `Published estimates put a wood fence at ${lifespan.wood.range} (Angi) and a well-built concrete block wall at ${lifespan.block.range} (HomeAdvisor). Vinyl falls in between at ${lifespan.vinyl.range} (Angi).`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I build a solid block wall in my front yard in St. George?',
    answer: 'Only within limits. St. George City Code §10-18-1 generally caps front-yard fences and walls at 4 feet, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only decorative fences that are at least 50 percent open are allowed, which a solid block wall is not. Any fence or wall in a front setback also needs a permit under §10-18-5.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which is better for privacy and noise?',
    answer: 'A solid, gap-free wall. The Federal Highway Administration notes that a noise barrier works best when it is long, continuous, and solid with few openings, which describes a mortared block wall better than a board fence that develops gaps as it ages.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often does a wood fence need sealing?',
    answer: `HomeGuide says wood needs sealing every 1 to 2 years. Staining or sealing a fence costs about ${fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot, or ${fenceStain.typical} for a typical 100 to 150 foot fence (HomeGuide, February 2026).`,
  },
];

const chooseBlock = [
  { icon: 'clock', title: 'You plan to stay for decades', text: `Block's ${lifespan.block.range} estimated life spreads its higher price over far more years.` },
  { icon: 'shield', title: 'You want the most privacy and sound control', text: 'A continuous, solid wall blocks sight lines and noise better than boards that shrink and gap.' },
  { icon: 'slope', title: 'The wall also holds soil', text: 'Where there is a grade change, a reinforced block wall can do the retaining job; wood cannot. See the retaining wall guide.' },
  { icon: 'sun', title: 'You do not want yearly upkeep', text: 'Block does not need sealing every year or two the way wood does.' },
];

const chooseWood = [
  { icon: 'tag', title: 'Budget is the deciding factor', text: `At ${wood.range} per foot, wood costs a fraction of a block wall today.` },
  { icon: 'ruler', title: 'You need it in a front yard near the sidewalk', text: 'An open picket or rail design can meet the city\'s 50-percent-open rule where a solid wall cannot.' },
  { icon: 'wrench', title: 'You want easy repairs', text: 'Boards and posts can be replaced one at a time without a mason.' },
  { icon: 'fence', title: 'It is a temporary or interior fence', text: 'Dog runs, garden enclosures, and short runs rarely justify a block footing.' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title="CMU vs Wood Fence, St. George UT"
  description="CMU block wall or wood fence in St. George, UT? Cost per foot, lifespan, upkeep, privacy, and the city rules that decide what you can build where."
  path={path}
  eyebrow="Comparison · Walls & fences · St. George & Washington County"
  h1="CMU Block Wall vs. Wood Fence in St. George: Cost, Lifespan, and Rules"
  answer={`As of ${COST_AS_OF}, a wood privacy fence costs about ${wood.range} per linear foot installed and lasts ${lifespan.wood.range}, while a 6-foot CMU block wall costs roughly ${blockWall.perLinear6ft} per linear foot and lasts ${lifespan.block.range}. Block costs more up front and far less to keep.`}
  updated="2026-09-26"
  updatedLabel="September 26, 2026"
  article={{ headline: 'CMU Block Wall vs. Wood Fence in St. George: Cost, Lifespan, and Rules', datePublished: '2026-09-26' }}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/block-wall-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Block wall installation', blurb: 'Types, permits, and vetting a mason.' },
    { href: '/fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence installation', blurb: 'Wood, vinyl, chain-link, and iron.' },
    { href: '/retaining-wall-cost-st-george-ut/', label: 'Retaining wall cost', blurb: 'When the wall also holds back soil.' },
    { href: '/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/', label: 'Full cost guide', blurb: 'Every range and source.' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    In St. George the choice between a block wall and a wood fence usually comes down to three things: how long you plan to
    stay, how much upkeep you will tolerate, and where the fence line runs, because the city's front-yard rules can decide the
    question for you. Here is how the two compare on the numbers, followed by the rules that apply to both.
  </p>

  <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
  <div class="sgfw-table-wrap">
    <table class="sgfw-table">
      <caption>CMU block wall vs. wood fence, as of {COST_AS_OF}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Factor</th>
          <th scope="col">CMU block wall</th>
          <th scope="col">Wood privacy fence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Installed cost</th>
          <td>{blockWall.perSqFt} per sq ft; about {blockWall.perLinear6ft} per linear ft at 6 ft tall</td>
          <td>{wood.range} per linear ft</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Estimated lifespan</th>
          <td>{lifespan.block.range} (HomeAdvisor)</td>
          <td>{lifespan.wood.range} (Angi)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Routine upkeep</th>
          <td>Occasional cleaning; patch or repaint any stucco or paint finish</td>
          <td>Sealing every 1 to 2 years (HomeGuide), about {fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear ft</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Privacy and noise</th>
          <td>Solid and continuous</td>
          <td>Good when new; gaps open as boards shrink</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Can it retain soil?</th>
          <td>Yes, when designed and reinforced as a retaining wall</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Permit in St. George</th>
          <td>Required in a front setback; required for CMU retaining walls 4 ft or taller (§10-18-5)</td>
          <td>Required in a front setback (§10-18-5)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Vinyl sits between the two: about {vinyl.range} per linear foot and {lifespan.vinyl.range} of life (Angi), though Angi notes
    UV exposure can make vinyl more brittle over time.
  </p>

  <h2>The real cost over time</h2>
  <p>
    Wood's lower price comes with a maintenance bill. HomeGuide says a wood fence needs sealing every one to two years, and
    staining or sealing a typical 100 to 150 foot fence costs about {fenceStain.typical}. Over a block wall's much longer life, a
    wood fence may also need to be replaced outright at least once. Block asks for more money on day one and very little after
    that, provided it was built on a proper footing with reinforcement where the design calls for it.
  </p>

  <h2>When a block wall makes more sense</h2>
  <IconList items={chooseBlock} />

  <h2>When a wood fence makes more sense</h2>
  <IconList items={chooseWood} />

  <h2>Rules that apply to both in St. George</h2>
  <p>
    A block wall used as a boundary or privacy wall is treated as a fence for height purposes. Under St. George City Code
    §10-18-1, most zones cap front-yard fences at 4 feet and side and rear-yard fences at 6 feet 4 inches, and within 10 feet
    behind the sidewalk only decorative fences that are at least 50 percent open, such as picket or wrought iron, are allowed.
    A permit is needed for any fence or retaining wall in a front setback, and for CMU and other retaining walls 4 feet or taller
    (§10-18-5). The <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> covers the details.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neighboring cities differ. Ivins, for example, requires CMU block colored "Sandstone" for walls along its arterial and
    collector streets (Ivins City Code 16.11.135). HOAs often add their own material and color rules on top of city code, so
    check your CC&amp;Rs before choosing; see the <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions to ask either kind of company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>For block: footing size and depth, rebar and grout spacing, cap style, and the finish (bare, painted, or stucco).</li>
    <li>For wood: species and grade, post material and depth, whether posts are set in concrete, and the first sealing.</li>
    <li>For both: who pulls the permit, how the property line was confirmed, and what the warranty covers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Sources: cost figures from the <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a>'s source list; lifespans from
    <a href={lifespan.wood.url} rel="noopener">Angi on wood fences</a> and <a href={lifespan.vinyl.url} rel="noopener">vinyl fences</a>
    (both updated July 9, 2026) and <a href={lifespan.block.url} rel="noopener">HomeAdvisor on block walls</a> (updated June 20, 2026);
    upkeep from <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/fence-installation-cost" rel="noopener">HomeGuide's fence cost guide</a>
    (February 3, 2026) and <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/cost-to-stain-paint-fence" rel="noopener">stain cost guide</a>
    (February 4, 2026); noise from the
    <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/ENVIRonment/noise/noise_compatible_planning/federal_approach/audible_landscape/al04.cfm" rel="noopener">Federal Highway Administration</a>
    (guidance on highway noise barriers); codes checked September 26, 2026.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
